  "image_name": "6640e5e735cbd17605264f2d604d4054.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a process flow for recycling PET waste into its original chemical building blocks. Cleaned PET flakes are first mechanically reduced in size through extrusion and cryo-grinding to form micronized PET powder, which is then fed to a hydrolysis reactor for enzymatic depolymerization. The reactor also receives enzyme and caustic solution, indicating controlled alkaline hydrolysis of the plastic.\n\nAfter reaction, the stream is clarified: solids are removed by filtration and sent to disposal, and the liquid passes through an activated carbon column, likely to remove color or impurities. The purified stream then enters a recovery section focused on separating valuable products and by-products. Acid and caustic additions are used around crystallization steps, suggesting pH adjustment to precipitate terephthalic acid. A membrane unit and a salt crystallizer split off waste and recover sodium sulfate as a co-product. A distillation column recovers ethylene glycol, while a water recycle loop returns water back upstream.\n\nOverall, the figure shows an integrated chemical-biological recycling scheme in which PET is broken down enzymatically and then separated into recycled terephthalic acid, ethylene glycol, and sodium sulfate, with water reused within the process.",
